-- Wikimedia Commons and loc.gov were unreachable from the newsroom sandbox on this run. Photo URLs are original-file Commons URLs built from file names found in web search (MD5 path); licenses were read from Commons file-page mirrors. Editor: confirm each photo loads and matches its caption; the image check removes any that do not.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261107-miller-chief-of-staff', 'trump-names-stephen-miller-chief-of-staff-replacing-susie-wiles-cabinet-changes', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'white-house', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Trump names Stephen Miller White House chief of staff, replacing Susie Wiles, and says "a few changes" to his Cabinet may follow', 'Trump announced the change on Friday, Nov. 6, three days after Republicans lost the House and the Senate. Miller takes over on Monday, Nov. 9, and Wiles moves to the president''s political operation.', 'President Trump named Stephen Miller as his White House chief of staff on Friday, Nov. 6, replacing Susie Wiles. Trump announced the move in a social media post, ABC News reported.

Miller, who has been deputy chief of staff, takes over on Monday, Nov. 9, according to Fox News. In his post, Trump called Miller one of his "most trusted advisors." He said Wiles is moving to his political operation and thanked her, saying she had "done a great job," Fox News reported.

## Wiles steps aside

Wiles said Friday was her last day as chief of staff and called the job "the honor of a lifetime." She said her role "may be changing" but that her commitment to the president would not, according to Fox News. A White House official told Fox News Digital that the decision was "very amicable" and that Wiles had submitted her resignation on Thursday night, Nov. 5.

Wiles served 21 months, the longest of any of Trump''s chiefs of staff across his two terms, and was the first woman to hold the job, Fox News noted. She also worked on his 2016 and 2020 campaigns.

## More changes ahead

The shake-up comes three days after the Tuesday, Nov. 3, midterms, in which Democrats won control of both chambers of Congress. Trump said in an interview with CBS News'' *60 Minutes*, recorded on Friday and set to air on Sunday, Nov. 8, that he loves his Cabinet but would make "maybe a few changes," The Nation reported. Asked whether he planned to fire anyone, he answered, "a little bit."

On Wednesday, Nov. 4, CBS News reported that Trump had asked advisers whether Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth should stay. Trump said that day that he thought Hegseth was "doing a very good job." Harmeet Dhillon, who led the Justice Department''s Civil Rights Division, resigned on Thursday, Nov. 5, according to Attorney General Todd Blanche, CBS News reported.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/The_office_lobby_in_the_White_House_LCCN96515467.jpg', 'The office lobby of the White House, photographed by Frances Benjamin Johnston, about 1889 (file). Photo: Library of Congress via Wikimedia Commons, public domain', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-07.sql', 9, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-07T10:50:00.000Z', '2026-11-07T10:50:00.000Z');
