INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261107-lame-duck', 'lame-duck-congress-returns-november-9-funding-deadline-december-11-defense-debt-limit', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'congress', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Lame-duck Congress returns on Monday, Nov. 9, facing a Dec. 11 funding deadline before Democrats take over', 'Republicans hold both chambers until the new Congress is sworn in on Sunday, Jan. 3. On the list: government funding, a $1.5 trillion defense request, the debt limit and President Trump''s health nominees.', 'Congress comes back on Monday, Nov. 9, for a lame-duck session. It is the last stretch in which Republicans control both chambers before Democrats take over when the new Congress is sworn in on Sunday, Jan. 3, HNGN reported. The session is expected to run until Christmas, with a break for Thanksgiving.

## The funding deadline

The biggest task is keeping the government open. The stopgap passed in September runs out on Friday, Dec. 11. HNGN noted that a lapse would be the fourth government shutdown of Trump''s second term. Lawmakers could pass an omnibus bill covering all 12 annual spending bills, or another short-term extension that would leave the fight to the incoming Democratic House.

STAT reported that any health care legislation passed this year would likely have to ride on that funding bill, and that Republicans want to confirm several of Trump''s Health and Human Services nominees before Democrats take control of the Senate.

## Other items on the list

- **Defense:** The administration wants a record $1.5 trillion for the military, which Senate Democrats reject, according to HNGN. The annual defense authorization bill is also pending.
- **Debt limit:** Republicans are weighing an increase before Democrats take over, which would remove a borrowing deadline that analysts see as possible leverage against Trump.
- **Other bills:** a possible farm bill, expiring health programs and resolutions to overturn federal rules.

## What comes next

House Republicans pick a new leader on Tuesday, Nov. 10, with Steve Scalise and Jim Jordan running after Speaker Mike Johnson declined to seek the post. Senate Majority Leader John Thune said Senate Republicans "will not stand idly by" as Democrats try to undo their work, HNGN reported.

House Democratic leader Hakeem Jeffries has outlined an agenda for the party''s first 100 hours in the majority that includes restoring enhanced Affordable Care Act subsidies, reversing Medicaid cuts and undoing Trump''s tariffs. Senate Democrats, with at least 54 seats, are expected to make Chuck Schumer majority leader.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Flickr_-_USCapitol_-_Progress_of_Civilization_-_Senate_Pediment.jpg/1280px-Flickr_-_USCapitol_-_Progress_of_Civilization_-_Senate_Pediment.jpg', 'The "Progress of Civilization" pediment over the Senate wing of the U.S. Capitol (file). Photo: Architect of the Capitol via Wikimedia Commons, public domain', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-07.sql', 7, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-07T10:50:00.000Z', '2026-11-07T10:50:00.000Z');
